Baseten's deepseek-ultrafast runs DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash at {{ K.meas4.decode_tok_s|n }} tok/s on 4x GB300. I modeled where the time goes.

About half of each 3.3 ms decode step isn't moving weights. It's fixed per-stage latency: ~{{ (K.fixed4_ms*1000/43)|n(0) }} µs per transformer block, waiting on dependent kernels and all-reduces. 🧵 [step breakdown]

**2/**
Their TP8 result is the tell. If the step were all bandwidth, 8 GPUs would be close to 2x faster than 4. They're {{ K.tp8_gain|pct }} faster. More GPUs shrink the streaming; the latency stays.

Same reason Rubin's {{ K.rubin_bw_ratio|n(1) }}x bandwidth buys only ~{{ (K.rubin_vs_gb300*100)|n(0) }}% with today's per-stage latency (modeled).

**3/**
The fix is an execution model, not more silicon. One persistent program with flag hand-offs instead of dependent kernel launches, and the next layers' weights streaming while this layer's experts load.

Same 4x GB300, modeled: {{ C.gpu_df_tp4.tok_s_user|n }} tok/s/user, {{ K.sw_vs_meas|x }}. [ablation]

**4/**
Then I designed the silicon anyway. MDA-1: a decode ASIC with the same 8 TB/s HBM, 2 PF FP8, a hardware MoE router (RTL-verified) and in-fabric all-reduce.

It runs at {{ pct(C.asic.tok_s_user / K.roofline) }} of its HBM roofline: {{ C.asic.tok_s_user|n }} tok/s/user. Same as good software on the same bandwidth. Silicon doesn't buy speed here.

**5/**
What it buys is economics: ~{{ K.usd_vs_sw|x }} lower $/token and ~{{ K.tpj_vs_sw|x }} tokens/J vs GPUs running that same software. That's at $45K/GPU and 60% TDP; the range is in the repo.

Break-even is ~{{ K.be_sw_300|n(-2) }} chips at $300M NRE. Hyperscaler math, not startup math.

**6/**
Things that surprised me:
• A 128x128 systolic array runs these decode GEMVs at 0.3-1.5% utilization (SCALE-Sim). The engine has to be 16 rows wide.
• SRAM expert caches are a dead end. They need GBs per chip.
• Engram's 203 GB of tables serve ~{{ (W.engram.lookup_bytes_per_step / (K.meas4.step_ms/1e3) / 1e6)|n(0) }} MB/s of lookups. Put them in LPDDR.
• An adversarial review caught two of my own mistakes, including a layout "inefficiency" in their engine that wasn't real. Both are fixed, and the list is in the repo.

