## LinkedIn

Baseten open-sourced deepseek-ultrafast, a hand-tuned engine that decodes DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash at {{ K.meas4.decode_tok_s|n }} tokens/s per user on 4x GB300. I used it as the reference workload for a hardware study, and the answer surprised me.

About half of every decode step is fixed per-stage latency, not memory bandwidth. A dataflow-style execution model removes most of it: on the same GPUs, the model projects {{ K.sw_vs_meas|x }} the speed. A custom decode ASIC built around that execution model reaches the same speed at the same memory bandwidth. Its advantage is cost and energy per token at volume, not raw speed.

The simulator, calibration against Baseten's published numbers, the joint uncertainty analysis and a verified RTL block are all in the repo, reproducible with one command. All projections are modeled.

## Reply to the Baseten team

Hi Baseten team, thanks for open-sourcing deepseek-ultrafast. It's the most legible serving engine I've read, and it made a serious hardware study possible.

I built an op-level performance model from the engine's source and V4.1's checkpoint headers. It accounts for PDL, the producer-pushed NVLS all-reduces, side streams, and what the `sgl` tensor maps actually stream. Fitted to your README's step times (3.30 ms TP4, 2.71 ms TP8), it attributes ~{{ K.fixed4_ms|n(1) }} ms of each TP4 step ({{ K.fixed4_share|pct }}; {{ K.fixed_lo|pct }}-{{ K.fixed_hi|pct }} across alternative fits) to dependent-stage latency, not weight streaming.

Three things you might find useful:

1. **Software projection.** On the same 4x GB300 the model projects about {{ C.gpu_df_tp4.tok_s_user|n }} tok/s/user from:
   - one persistent program with flag hand-offs, assuming a stage hand-off can get near 1 µs (the least certain part);
   - L2 prefetch of the next layers' dense weights while the current layer's experts stream.

   The prefetch piece is the least obvious. A layer's gate→expert gap is exactly when the next layers' attention and shared-expert weights can stream.
2. **TP8 scaling.** The model attributes the weak TP4→TP8 scaling to per-stage latency, not bandwidth. At TP8 only w2's 64-row padding and the shared expert's padding are streamed, a few percent.
3. **One profile settles it.** `tools/model_vs_nsys.py` prints the model's prediction in the exact groups of your `tools/nsys_breakdown.py`. One `nsys` run would confirm or kill the diagnosis. I'd love to compare notes if you're up for sharing a breakdown.

The ASIC side (MDA-1) is a simulated projection. It matches that software projection and wins only on $/token and W/token at volume. Everything is here: https://github.com/benblaine/dsv41-accel-study
